    "q-126570": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126570",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{x}{3}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2}{3}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
